# Clean Code

## Objective

Produce code that is easy to read, easy to change, and free of unnecessary indirection or cleverness.

## Core Principles

- Names reveal intent: no `data2`, `tmp`, `handleStuff`.
- Functions do one thing; if a name needs 'and', split it.
- Prefer straightforward control flow over clever one-liners.
- Comments explain *why*, not *what* — the code should already say what.

## Requirements

- Keep functions short enough to read without scrolling; extract when they grow.
- Remove dead code and commented-out blocks instead of leaving them 'just in case'.
- Match the existing formatting/style of the file being edited, not a personal preference.
- Avoid deep nesting; prefer early returns / guard clauses.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Introducing a new abstraction layer for a single use case.
- Renaming unrelated variables/files as a side effect of an unrelated change.
- Adding comments that restate the code instead of explaining intent.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ A new reader could understand the function's purpose without external context.
✓ No unused variables, imports, or dead branches were left behind.
✓ Formatting/style matches the surrounding codebase.

## Golden Rule

Code is read far more often than it is written — optimize for the reader.
